POL-AML-001|Structuring & Smurfing Detection|1|Purpose|1.1 This policy sets how the Bank detects and escalates deposits structured to avoid the cash reporting threshold.
POL-AML-001|Structuring & Smurfing Detection|2|Reporting Threshold|2.1 The cash transaction reporting threshold is INR 2,00,000 (₹2L) per transaction. 2.2 Deposits at or above the threshold are reported through the Cash Transaction Report process and are not treated as structuring on that basis alone.
POL-AML-001|Structuring & Smurfing Detection|3|Structuring Pattern Definition|3.1 Structuring is presumed where an account receives three (3) or more cash deposits each between ₹1,80,000 and ₹1,99,999 within any rolling ten (10) day window. 3.2 The pattern applies per account. Deposits through different channels (BRANCH, ATM, MOBILE, ONLINE) are aggregated.
POL-AML-001|Structuring & Smurfing Detection|4|Escalation|4.1 A structuring alert must be escalated to the Compliance Officer within 24 hours of detection. 4.2 The alert must stay open until a documented disposition is recorded.
POL-AML-001|Structuring & Smurfing Detection|4.5|RING_PATTERN: Coordinated Activity Across Accounts|4.5.1 Three (3) or more distinct accounts transacting with the same counterparty or device within a 72-hour window, absent a legitimate shared business relationship, are presumptively treated as coordinated activity requiring escalation under Clause 4.1. 4.5.2 A legitimate shared business relationship includes widely used utility, tax, payroll or merchant counterparties paying or paid by many unrelated customers. Such counterparties are excluded from ring treatment. 4.5.3 Each account in a ring is individually reviewed; the ring finding is documented as one case that references all member accounts and transaction IDs.
POL-AML-001|Structuring & Smurfing Detection|5|Evidentiary Standard|5.1 A finding must cite the account ID, each transaction ID forming the pattern, the amounts, and the dates. 5.2 Findings that cannot be tied to specific transaction IDs must not be recorded as confirmed.
POL-AML-001|Structuring & Smurfing Detection|6|Record Retention|6.1 Structuring alerts, supporting transaction records and the documented disposition must be retained for at least five (5) years from the date of the transaction or closure of the account, whichever is later.
POL-AML-002|High-Risk Jurisdiction Transactions|1|Purpose|1.1 This policy governs transactions to or from jurisdictions identified by the Financial Action Task Force (FATF) as high-risk.
POL-AML-002|High-Risk Jurisdiction Transactions|2|Definition|2.1 A high-risk jurisdiction is any country on the FATF "black list" (call for action) or "grey list" (increased monitoring), as maintained in the Bank's Jurisdiction Register. 2.2 For this Bank's current register, this includes Myanmar, North Korea, Iran and Panama.
POL-AML-002|High-Risk Jurisdiction Transactions|3|Enhanced Due Diligence|3.1 Enhanced Due Diligence (EDD) is mandatory for any remittance or transfer of ₹5,00,000 (₹5L) or more to or from a high-risk jurisdiction. 3.2 EDD requires documented purpose of payment, beneficiary verification, and source of funds. 3.3 A transaction above the threshold with no EDD record on file is non-compliant.
POL-AML-002|High-Risk Jurisdiction Transactions|4|Escalation|4.1 Every transaction under Clause 3.1 must be escalated to the Compliance Officer on the same working day, whether or not EDD is completed.
POL-AML-002|High-Risk Jurisdiction Transactions|5|Evidentiary Standard|5.1 A finding must cite the transaction ID, amount, counterparty country and date, and state whether an EDD record exists.
POL-AML-002|High-Risk Jurisdiction Transactions|6|Record Retention|6.1 Jurisdiction screening results, EDD files and escalation records must be retained for at least five (5) years from the date of the transaction.
POL-AML-003|Transaction Velocity Monitoring|1|Purpose|1.1 This policy addresses rapid pass-through of funds, a common layering indicator.
POL-AML-003|Transaction Velocity Monitoring|2|Velocity Pattern Definition|2.1 A velocity pattern exists where an account receives a deposit of ₹5,00,000 or more and 80% or more of that amount leaves the account by withdrawal or transfer within 24 hours of the deposit.
POL-AML-003|Transaction Velocity Monitoring|3|Automatic Hold|3.1 On detection, the Bank may place an automatic hold on further outward movement from the account pending review. 3.2 A hold must be reviewed by the Compliance Officer within 24 hours.
POL-AML-003|Transaction Velocity Monitoring|4|Escalation|4.1 A velocity alert must be escalated to the Compliance Officer within 24 hours of detection.
POL-AML-003|Transaction Velocity Monitoring|5|Evidentiary Standard|5.1 A finding must cite the account ID, the deposit transaction ID and amount, each outgoing transaction ID and amount, the elapsed hours, and the computed percentage moved out.
POL-AML-003|Transaction Velocity Monitoring|6|Record Retention|6.1 Velocity alerts, hold decisions and the related transaction records must be retained for at least five (5) years from the date of the transaction.
POL-AML-004|STR Filing Timelines & KYC Obligations|1|Purpose|1.1 This policy sets timelines for filing a Suspicious Transaction Report (STR) with FIU-IND and KYC dependencies.
POL-AML-004|STR Filing Timelines & KYC Obligations|2|STR Filing Timeline|2.1 Under Rule 8 of the PMLA Rules, an STR must be filed within seven (7) working days of the Principal Officer being satisfied that a transaction is suspicious. 2.2 Filing is always performed and signed off by a human Principal Officer. No system files an STR automatically.
POL-AML-004|STR Filing Timelines & KYC Obligations|3|KYC Dependency|3.1 Where the customer's KYC status is PENDING or EXPIRED, the case must be marked as a KYC control deficiency and is escalated alongside the STR assessment. 3.2 KYC must be refreshed before further high-value activity is permitted on the account.
POL-AML-004|STR Filing Timelines & KYC Obligations|4|Risk-Segment Escalation Multiplier|4.1 Cases on HIGH risk-segment customers are reviewed with priority: the internal review deadline is halved. 4.2 Cases on MEDIUM risk-segment customers follow the standard deadline.
POL-AML-004|STR Filing Timelines & KYC Obligations|5|Control Deficiency Reporting|5.1 Any missing EDD, expired KYC or missed escalation timeline must be recorded as a control deficiency and reported to the Compliance Committee at the next meeting.
POL-AML-004|STR Filing Timelines & KYC Obligations|6|Record Retention|6.1 STR copies, acknowledgements and KYC records must be retained for at least five (5) years after the end of the business relationship.
POL-CR-001|NPA Classification & Provisioning|1|Purpose|1.1 This policy sets Non-Performing Asset (NPA) classification and provisioning in line with RBI IRAC norms.
POL-CR-001|NPA Classification & Provisioning|2|NPA Threshold|2.1 A loan account is classified as an NPA when interest or principal is overdue for more than 90 days.
POL-CR-001|NPA Classification & Provisioning|3|Sub-classification|3.1 Substandard: NPA for up to 12 months. 3.2 Doubtful: NPA for more than 12 months. 3.3 Loss: asset identified as uncollectible.
POL-CR-001|NPA Classification & Provisioning|4|Provisioning|4.1 Substandard assets: provision of 15% of the outstanding amount. 4.2 Doubtful assets: provision of 25% to 100% of the outstanding amount depending on the age of the doubtful classification. 4.3 Loss assets: provision of 100% of the outstanding amount.
POL-CR-001|NPA Classification & Provisioning|5|Evidentiary Standard|5.1 A finding must cite the loan account ID, days past due, outstanding amount, the sub-classification, and the provision computed with the percentage applied.
POL-LIQ-001|Basel III Liquidity Coverage Ratio (LCR) Compliance|1|Purpose|1.1 This policy defines the Bank's LCR requirement and breach handling.
POL-LIQ-001|Basel III Liquidity Coverage Ratio (LCR) Compliance|2|Requirement|2.1 LCR = High Quality Liquid Assets (HQLA) ÷ Total Net Cash Outflows over the next 30 days. 2.2 The Bank must maintain an LCR of 100% or more at all times.
POL-LIQ-001|Basel III Liquidity Coverage Ratio (LCR) Compliance|3|Breach Classification and Escalation|3.1 Any daily LCR below 100% is a breach. 3.2 A breach must be escalated to the Asset-Liability Committee (ALCO) within 4 hours of detection.
POL-LIQ-001|Basel III Liquidity Coverage Ratio (LCR) Compliance|4|Remediation|4.1 A remediation plan must be documented, stating the shortfall in HQLA, the actions taken, and the date the LCR is restored to 100% or more.
POL-LIQ-001|Basel III Liquidity Coverage Ratio (LCR) Compliance|5|Evidentiary Standard|5.1 A finding must cite the snapshot date, HQLA amount, net cash outflows, and the computed LCR.